Tensor-core attention for prompt passes of the Qwen3.5 hybrids on CUDA (bf16 cache).

Stock 1.1.1 runs a prompt pass's attention through the decode kernel (tf_q35_attn_partial): every row of a pass gets
its own blocks and reads all cached keys and values once per row, on CUDA cores (~1.5 TFLOPS on a GB10). Prefill of
Ornith-1.5-122B fell from ~600 tok/s at 2K to 107 tok/s at 98K tokens, almost all of it this kernel at depth.
qwen35_prompt_attn.cuh adds tf_q35_attn_prompt: a block takes 8 consecutive chain rows (a warp each, its 16 query
heads of one key head as the 16 rows of an mma tile), loads each 16-key tile of keys and values into shared memory
once for the 128 queries (double-buffered), and computes S = Q K^T and O = P V with mma.sync m16n8k16 (bf16 in, fp32
sums) and an online softmax; key splits (1 for passes of 256+ rows, else 4) are merged by tf_q35_attn_prompt_merge
into the same bf16 rows attn_merge writes. 96 KiB dynamic shared memory (opted into at engine init).
cuda_forward.zig uses it only for prompt passes (one segment, bf16 cache, 16 query heads a key head); verify windows,
decode rows and --kv-cache fp8 keep the stock kernels, so drafted replies still equal "draft": false.
TENSORFOLD_PROMPT_ATTN=0 restores the stock kernel for prompts.
Verified: against the stock kernels on one layer (tools/pa_test.cu, 128 and 512 rows, depths 0-100K) the outputs
differ by bf16 rounding only (max |d| 0.0078, no non-finite values) and the layer runs 10x (2K) to 24x (100K) faster
(~35 TFLOPS at 100K keys). End to end: README.

